{
    "esm": "1.0.0",
    "metadata": {
        "name": "NOxPhotochemistry",
        "description": "The NOx (NO + NO₂) photochemical cycle that governs tropospheric ozone — Seinfeld & Pandis (2006) Ch. 6 §6.2, Eqs. 6.5-6.8, pp. 207-212. During daytime three reactions cycle rapidly between NO, NO₂ and O₃: NO₂ + hν → NO + O (λ < 424 nm), O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M, and NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂. In the absence of other oxidants this cycle reaches a photostationary state (the Leighton relationship). Implements four algebraic diagnostic relations: (Eq. 6.5) ground-state O-atom steady-state concentration O = j_NO2·[NO₂] / (k_O_O2_M·[O₂]·[M]), (Eq. 6.6) photostationary-state ozone O3_pss = j_NO2·[NO₂] / (k_NO_O3·[NO]) — the Leighton relationship, (Eq. 6.7) the photostationary-state parameter Φ = j_NO2·[NO₂] / (k_NO_O3·[NO]·[O₃]) which is 1 at exact PSS and deviates when peroxy radicals perturb the cycle, and (Eq. 6.8) the net ozone production rate P_O3 = j_NO2·[NO₂] − k_NO_O3·[NO]·[O₃]. Observed-only algebraic system (no time derivatives): nine variables (five inputs NO/NO2/O3/O2/M encoded as parameters, four outputs O/O3_pss/Φ/P_O3 encoded as state) plus three rate-constant parameters (j_NO2, k_O_O2_M, k_NO_O3). Migrated from GasChem.jl@8c12c048 src/nox_photochemistry.jl:88 (`NOxPhotochemistry` @component). Sibling component PhotostationaryState (src/nox_photochemistry.jl:145) migrates separately to photostationary.esm.",
        "tags": [
            "gaschem",
            "nox-photochemistry",
            "tropospheric-ozone",
            "photostationary-state",
            "leighton-relationship",
            "observed-only",
            "seinfeld-pandis"
        ],
        "created": "2026-05-17T00:00:00Z",
        "authors": [
            "GasChem.jl contributors",
            "EarthSciModels migration (esm-nw73)"
        ],
        "references": [
            {
                "citation": "Seinfeld, J. H., & Pandis, S. N. (2006). Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics: From Air Pollution to Climate Change, 2nd ed. Wiley. Chapter 6 §6.2, Eqs. 6.5-6.8, pp. 207-212. The NO-NO₂-O₃ photochemical cycle, the photostationary-state (Leighton) relationship, the photostationary-state parameter Φ, and the net ozone production rate. Rate constants from Table B.1; j_NO2 typical midday value from Figure 4.x photolysis-rate discussion."
            }
        ]
    },
    "models": {
        "NOxPhotochemistry": {
            "reference": {
                "notes": "source_ref: GasChem.jl@8c12c048482b515fb8eb2110bf8ab4b4f4e71309:src/nox_photochemistry.jl:88\n\nUpstream `NOxPhotochemistry` is an MTK `@component` with 9 `@variables` (5 inputs NO/NO2/O3/O2/M + 4 outputs O/O3_pss/Φ/P_O3), 3 `@parameters` (j_NO2, k_O_O2_M, k_NO_O3), 0 `@constants`, and 4 algebraic equations from S&P §6.2 (Eqs. 6.5-6.8). It returns `System(eqs, t; name)` — a system carrying the time independent variable `t` but with no `D(·)` time-derivative operator in any equation; the upstream tests and doc page compile it with `mtkcompile(sys; inputs=[NO, NO2, O3, O2, M])` and solve it as a `NonlinearProblem` with the five inputs pinned to user-supplied concentrations.\n\nVariable-classification convention: the five input @variables (NO, NO2, O3, O2, M) are encoded here as `type: parameter` because they have no defining equation within the NOxPhotochemistry component itself — they are externally-supplied inputs that the upstream compile treats via `inputs=[...]` to NonlinearProblem. This mirrors the rig-wide convention applied by sibling oh_production.esm (the upstream input @variables O₃/H₂O/M are encoded as parameters) and typical.esm/urban.esm/remote.esm (upstream air-composition species encoded as parameters). The four output @variables (O, O3_pss, Φ, P_O3) are encoded as `type: state` because each has exactly one defining algebraic equation that the inline-test runner solves as part of the post-mtkcompile observed-binding layer. The upstream structural test `NOxPhotochemistry: Structural Verification` (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl:4-31) asserts `length(unknowns(sys)) == 9`, `length(equations(sys)) == 4`, and `length(parameters(sys)) == 3` — the .esm preserves this with the 9 declared variables (5 parameter + 4 state), 4 entries in the `equations` array, and 3 rate-constant parameters.\n\nObservability classification: this component is purely algebraic — every output variable is the LHS of one algebraic equation in the inputs and rate-constant parameters (no derivative; the `(t)` time index has no role since there is no `D(·)` operator in any equation). Test scenarios use `time_span: [0.0, 1.0]` and sample at t=0 to mirror the convention used by sibling observed-only ESM files (oh_production.esm, gwp.esm, photon_energy.esm, stefan_boltzmann.esm, wien.esm). For an algebraic-only system the trajectory is constant in time and converges to the same fixed point in one nonlinear-solver step regardless of the sampling time.\n\nRate-constant CGS→SI translation: the upstream Julia declares the rate constants as SI-converted CGS values via explicit multiplication:\n  j_NO2     = 8.0e-3 s⁻¹                  (typical midday NO₂ photolysis rate, S&P §6.2; first-order, no conversion)\n  k_O_O2_M  = 6.0e-34 · 1.0e-12 m⁶/s      (O + O₂ + M → O₃ termolecular; CGS 6.0e-34 cm⁶/molec²/s × 1e-12 cm⁶→m⁶ factor)\n  k_NO_O3   = 1.9e-14 · 1.0e-6  m³/s      (NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂ bimolecular; CGS 1.9e-14 cm³/molec/s × 1e-6 cm³→m³ factor, S&P p. 211)\nThe products evaluate in IEEE-754 Float64 to exactly the clean literals: `6.0e-34 * 1.0e-12 == 6.0e-46` and `1.9e-14 * 1.0e-6 == 1.9e-20` (both bit-identical — verified), so the .esm encodes the SI defaults directly as 6.0e-46 and 1.9e-20.\n\nInput-variable defaults: the five input @variables carry no upstream default (MTK @variables, unlike @parameters, have no `= value`). The .esm assigns each input a `default` taken from the upstream test's typical-midday scenario (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl `NOxPhotochemistry: Eq 6.5/6.6` testitems, lines 54-111): NO = 2.5e16 m⁻³ (~1 ppb at STP), NO2 = 5.0e16 m⁻³ (~2 ppb), O3 = 1.0e18 m⁻³ (~40 ppb tropospheric background), O2 = 5.25e24 m⁻³ (21% of STP air number density), M = 2.5e25 m⁻³ (STP total air number density). This matches the rig convention used by sibling oh_production.esm (input defaults from the upstream test typical scenario).\n\nEquation structure: the four outputs are mutually independent — each depends only on inputs and rate-constant parameters, with no inter-output references (unlike the oh_production.esm cascade where P_OH references ε_OH). All four share the numerator j_NO2·NO2. Eq. 6.5 (O) and Eqs. 6.6/6.7/6.8 use distinct denominators. O3_pss (Eq. 6.6) is independent of the O3 input; Φ (Eq. 6.7) and P_O3 (Eq. 6.8) depend on O3. At the typical-midday defaults O3_pss = 8.42e17 m⁻³, which is below the default O3 input of 1.0e18 m⁻³, so the default Φ = 0.842 < 1 and P_O3 = -7.5e13 m⁻³/s < 0 (net ozone loss — the default O3 sits above its photostationary value).\n\nNumerical defaults (S&P typical-midday surface conditions, SI):\n  At NO=2.5e16, NO2=5.0e16, O3=1.0e18, O2=5.25e24, M=2.5e25:\n  O      = 8.0e-3·5.0e16 / (6.0e-46·5.25e24·2.5e25) = 5.079365e9 m⁻³  (short-lived ground-state O atom)\n  O3_pss = 8.0e-3·5.0e16 / (1.9e-20·2.5e16)        = 8.421053e17 m⁻³ (~34 ppb — the Leighton value; S&P hand-calc ≈ 8.42e17)\n  Φ      = 8.0e-3·5.0e16 / (1.9e-20·2.5e16·1.0e18)  = 0.8421053       (< 1: default O3 exceeds its PSS value)\n  P_O3   = 8.0e-3·5.0e16 − 1.9e-20·2.5e16·1.0e18    = -7.5e13 m⁻³/s   (< 0: net ozone loss at the default state)\n\nTolerance rationale (model-level rel=1.0e-10 / abs=1.0e-12): the four algebraic equations are small expression trees (2-4 IEEE-754 ops each — Eq. 6.5 is two multiplications + one division for the numerator/denominator plus the joining division; Eq. 6.8 is two multiplications + one subtraction). Each output is correctly-rounded to within ~1 ULP ≈ 1e-16 relative; rel=1.0e-10 leaves 6 orders of magnitude of headroom over the IEEE-754 floor and is comfortably tighter than the upstream rtol bounds (rtol=0.05 for the Eq. 6.6 hand-calc cross-check, rtol=1.0e-6 for the Φ-at-PSS and O3_pss-linearity identities). The abs=1.0e-12 floor handles the P_O3-at-photostationary-state boundary case where the j_NO2·NO2 − k_NO_O3·NO·O3 near-cancellation collapses to IEEE-754 exact zero. Matches the convention used by sibling observed-only ESM files: oh_production.esm at rel=1e-10/abs=1e-12, stefan_boltzmann.esm at rel=1e-10/abs=1e-12, wien.esm at rel=1e-10, gwp.esm at rel=1e-10.\n\nUnit balance (verified): all four equations balance dimensionally.\n  Eq. 6.5 `O ~ j_NO2·NO2 / (k_O_O2_M·O2·M)`: (s⁻¹·m⁻³) / ((m⁶/s)·m⁻³·m⁻³) = (m⁻³/s) / (s⁻¹) = m⁻³ ✓\n  Eq. 6.6 `O3_pss ~ j_NO2·NO2 / (k_NO_O3·NO)`: (s⁻¹·m⁻³) / ((m³/s)·m⁻³) = (m⁻³/s) / (s⁻¹) = m⁻³ ✓\n  Eq. 6.7 `Φ ~ j_NO2·NO2 / (k_NO_O3·NO·O3)`: (s⁻¹·m⁻³) / ((m³/s)·m⁻³·m⁻³) = (m⁻³/s) / (m⁻³/s) = dimensionless ✓\n  Eq. 6.8 `P_O3 ~ j_NO2·NO2 − k_NO_O3·NO·O3`: (s⁻¹·m⁻³) − ((m³/s)·m⁻³·m⁻³) = m⁻³·s⁻¹ − m⁻³·s⁻¹ = m⁻³·s⁻¹ ✓\nThe migrated .esm preserves the upstream units verbatim — all concentrations in SI m⁻³, j_NO2 in s⁻¹, k_NO_O3 in m³/s (bimolecular), k_O_O2_M in m⁶/s (termolecular), Φ dimensionless, P_O3 in m⁻³·s⁻¹. SI throughout; no unit conversion at parse or runtime.\n\nUpstream test reproduction (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl):\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: Structural Verification` (lines 4-31): 9 unknowns / 4 equations / 3 parameters and required output names O3_pss, P_O3 — reproduced by the .esm structural shape (5 parameter + 4 state variables under `variables`, 4 entries in `equations`).\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: Eq 6.5 O Atom Steady-State` (lines 54-79): asserts 0 < O < 1.0e12 at the typical scenario — reproduced and tightened by `typical_midday_all_outputs` pinning the exact O = 5079365079.365079 m⁻³ (well inside the 0..1e12 band), and by `o_atom_linear_in_j_no2` / `o_atom_inverse_in_air_density` exercising Eq. 6.5's structural linearity in j_NO2 and inverse dependence on M.\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: Eq 6.6 Leighton Relationship` (lines 81-111): asserts 1.0e17 < O3_pss < 1.0e19 and O3_pss ≈ 8.42e17 at rtol=0.05 — reproduced and tightened by `typical_midday_all_outputs` pinning the exact O3_pss = 8.421052631578948e17 m⁻³ at rel=1e-10.\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: Eq 6.7 Photostationary State Parameter` (lines 113-148): asserts Φ ≈ 1.0 at O3 = O3_pss (rtol=1e-6), Φ > 1 below PSS, Φ < 1 above PSS — reproduced by `phi_unity_at_photostationary_state` (Φ = 1.0 at O3 = O3_pss), `phi_above_unity_below_pss` (Φ = 2.0 at O3 = 0.5·O3_pss), `phi_below_unity_above_pss` (Φ = 0.5 at O3 = 2·O3_pss).\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: Eq 6.8 Net O3 Production` (lines 150-184): asserts |P_O3|/(j_NO2·NO2) < 1e-6 at PSS, P_O3 > 0 below PSS, P_O3 < 0 above PSS — reproduced by `phi_unity_at_photostationary_state` (P_O3 = 0 at O3 = O3_pss), `phi_above_unity_below_pss` (P_O3 = +2.0e14 below PSS), `phi_below_unity_above_pss` (P_O3 = -4.0e14 above PSS).\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: Rate Constants at 298 K` (lines 189-200): asserts the default values of j_NO2, k_O_O2_M, k_NO_O3 — implicitly covered by the `default` fields declared on each parameter; the structural-test gate verifies they are bit-identical to the upstream Julia values.\n- `NOxPhotochemistry: O3_pss Scales with NO2/NO Ratio` (lines 205-228): asserts O3_pss(NO2=2e16)/O3_pss(NO2=1e16) ≈ 2.0 at rtol=1e-6 — reproduced by `o3_pss_linear_in_no2_baseline` and `o3_pss_linear_in_no2_doubled` pinning both endpoints (4.210526315789474e17 and 8.421052631578948e17) at rel=1e-10 so the ratio is exact by IEEE-754 multiplication-by-2 reproducibility; `o3_pss_inverse_in_no` additionally pins the 1/[NO] dependence and `o3_pss_independent_of_o3_input` confirms Eq. 6.6's independence of the O3 input.\nThe upstream tests pin only qualitative bounds and one rtol=0.05 hand-calc; the migrated .esm tests upgrade these to quantitative value pins at rel=1e-10, a strict tightening — every upstream qualitative assertion is implied by the more-specific pinned value. Additional regime tests (`polluted_urban_high_nox`, `clean_remote_low_nox`) exercise all four outputs across the urban-to-remote NOx range for full regression coverage."
            },
            "tolerance": {
                "rel": 1e-10,
                "abs": 1e-12
            },
            "variables": {
                "j_NO2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "s^-1",
                    "default": 0.008,
                    "description": "NO₂ photolysis rate j_{NO₂} [s⁻¹] for NO₂ + hν → NO + O (λ < 424 nm). The upstream default 8.0e-3 s⁻¹ is the canonical typical-midday surface value (S&P §6.2) — clear-sky, solar zenith angle near noon. j_NO2 is the engine of the whole NOx cycle: it sets the rate of O-atom production (Eq. 6.5) and, together with k_NO_O3, fixes the photostationary-state ozone level (Eq. 6.6). Highly variable in practice — it drops to zero at night, scales with sec(SZA) through the day, and increases with altitude as the overhead O₃ column thins. Overridable for diurnal, seasonal, or altitude scenarios; in the full TroposphericChemistrySystem it is supplied by the upstream FastJX photolysis subsystem via param_to_var coupling."
                },
                "k_O_O2_M": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^6/s",
                    "default": 6e-46,
                    "description": "Termolecular rate constant for O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M [m⁶ s⁻¹]. The upstream default is the SI-converted CGS value 6.0e-34 cm⁶ molec⁻² s⁻¹ × 1e-12 (cm⁶→m⁶) = 6.0e-46 m⁶/s (the upstream Julia writes the literal product `6.0e-34 * 1.0e-12`, which evaluates to exactly 6.0e-46 in IEEE-754 Float64). This is the recombination step that converts the O atom from NO₂ photolysis into ozone; it appears only in Eq. 6.5 as part of the O-atom loss denominator k_O_O2_M·[O₂]·[M]. The very large quenching rate k_O_O2_M·[O₂]·[M] ≈ 7.9e4 s⁻¹ at STP makes the ground-state O atom extremely short-lived (~13 µs), which is why Eq. 6.5 is a valid steady-state relation."
                },
                "k_NO_O3": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^3/s",
                    "default": 1.9e-20,
                    "description": "Bimolecular rate constant for NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂ [m³ s⁻¹]. The upstream default is the SI-converted CGS value 1.9e-14 cm³ molec⁻¹ s⁻¹ × 1e-6 (cm³→m³) = 1.9e-20 m³/s (S&P p. 211; the upstream Julia writes the literal product `1.9e-14 * 1.0e-6`, exactly 1.9e-20 in Float64). This is the ozone-titration step that closes the NOx cycle by reconverting NO to NO₂. It sets the photostationary-state ozone level (Eq. 6.6: O3_pss ∝ j_NO2/k_NO_O3) and the ozone-loss term of the net production rate (Eq. 6.8). Weakly temperature-dependent in reality (Arrhenius); the upstream uses the temperature-independent ~298 K value for the algebraic system."
                },
                "NO": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 2.5e+16,
                    "description": "Nitric oxide number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 2.5e16 m⁻³ ≈ 1 ppb at STP is the upstream test's typical-midday value (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl:59). NO appears in the denominators of Eqs. 6.6/6.7 and the loss term of Eq. 6.8 — higher NO lowers the photostationary-state ozone (O3_pss ∝ 1/[NO]) and raises the O₃-titration loss. Encoded as `type: parameter` (not `type: state`) because NOxPhotochemistry has no governing equation for NO — it is purely an input. In the full TroposphericChemistrySystem NO is provided by the upstream NOx-emission and chemistry subsystems; in standalone tests it is overridden via `parameter_overrides`. Tropospheric range ~2.5e14 (remote, 0.01 ppb) to ~2.5e18 (polluted urban, 100 ppb)."
                },
                "NO2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 5e+16,
                    "description": "Nitrogen dioxide number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 5.0e16 m⁻³ ≈ 2 ppb at STP is the upstream test's typical-midday value (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl:60). NO₂ is the photolytic source of the cycle: it appears in the numerator j_NO2·[NO₂] of all four equations, so every output scales linearly with NO₂. The photostationary-state ozone is proportional to the [NO₂]/[NO] ratio (Eq. 6.6, the Leighton relationship). Encoded as `type: parameter` for the same reason as NO — it is an external input with no defining equation in this component."
                },
                "O3": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 1e+18,
                    "description": "Ozone number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input — the measured/ambient O₃, distinct from the diagnostic O3_pss output). The default 1.0e18 m⁻³ ≈ 40 ppb at STP is the upstream test's typical tropospheric background (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl:61). O3 enters Eq. 6.7 (Φ) and Eq. 6.8 (P_O3) — comparing the ambient O3 against the photostationary value O3_pss is exactly what Φ measures: Φ = O3_pss/O3, so Φ > 1 when ambient O3 is below its PSS value (net production) and Φ < 1 when above (net loss). O3 does NOT enter Eq. 6.6 — O3_pss is independent of the ambient O3 input. Encoded as `type: parameter`: an external input with no governing equation here."
                },
                "O2": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 5.25e+24,
                    "description": "Molecular oxygen number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 5.25e24 m⁻³ is 21% of the STP total air number density (2.5e25), i.e. the well-mixed atmospheric O₂ fraction. O₂ appears only in Eq. 6.5 as part of the O-atom quenching denominator k_O_O2_M·[O₂]·[M] (the O₂ is the reactant that recombines with the O atom to form O₃). Encoded as `type: parameter`: an external input. Like M it decreases with altitude in lockstep with total air density (the mixing ratio stays ~constant below the homopause)."
                },
                "M": {
                    "type": "parameter",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "default": 2.5e+25,
                    "description": "Total air number density [m⁻³] (externally-supplied input). The default 2.5e25 m⁻³ is the STP value used by the upstream test (test/test_nox_photochemistry.jl:63) and by sibling typical.esm. M is the third body in the O + O₂ + M → O₃ recombination and appears only in Eq. 6.5's O-atom quenching denominator. M decreases roughly exponentially with altitude (scale height ~8 km in the troposphere): ~1e25 at 12 km, ~1e24 at 30 km. Encoded as `type: parameter`: an external input supplied by the upstream transport/thermodynamics module."
                },
                "O": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "description": "Ground-state atomic oxygen O(³P) steady-state number density [m⁻³] (Eq. 6.5). Algebraic balance between production from NO₂ photolysis (j_NO2·[NO₂]) and loss by recombination with O₂ (k_O_O2_M·[O₂]·[M]): O = j_NO2·[NO₂] / (k_O_O2_M·[O₂]·[M]). At the typical-midday default conditions O ≈ 5.08e9 m⁻³ — extremely low, because the O atom is consumed within ~13 µs by the fast three-body recombination. The 'steady-state' label reflects the quasi-equilibrium assumption: the O-atom lifetime is far shorter than any other timescale in the cycle, so its concentration tracks the instantaneous NO₂ photolysis rate. Linear in j_NO2 and in [NO₂]; inversely proportional to [O₂] and [M]."
                },
                "O3_pss": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3",
                    "description": "Photostationary-state ozone number density [m⁻³] (Eq. 6.6 — the Leighton relationship). The ozone concentration the NO-NO₂-O₃ cycle would reach in pure photochemical equilibrium: O3_pss = j_NO2·[NO₂] / (k_NO_O3·[NO]). Proportional to the [NO₂]/[NO] ratio and to j_NO2/k_NO_O3. At the typical-midday defaults O3_pss ≈ 8.42e17 m⁻³ (~34 ppb). Independent of the ambient O3 input — O3_pss is what the cycle predicts, not what is measured. In real urban air the measured O3 deviates from O3_pss because peroxy radicals (HO₂, RO₂) provide an extra NO→NO₂ pathway beyond NO + O₃; that deviation is quantified by Φ."
                },
                "Φ": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "1",
                    "description": "Photostationary-state parameter Φ, dimensionless (Eq. 6.7). The ratio of the NO₂-photolysis ozone source to the NO+O₃ ozone sink: Φ = j_NO2·[NO₂] / (k_NO_O3·[NO]·[O₃]) = O3_pss/O3. Φ = 1 marks exact photostationary state (pure NO-NO₂-O₃ cycling, no net ozone change). Φ > 1 means the ambient O₃ is below its photostationary value — additional oxidants (HO₂, RO₂ peroxy radicals) are converting NO to NO₂ without consuming O₃, driving net ozone production; typical urban daytime measurements show Φ ≈ 1.5-3. Φ < 1 means ambient O₃ exceeds its PSS value — net ozone loss. At the typical-midday defaults Φ ≈ 0.842 (the default O3 input of 40 ppb sits above the 34 ppb photostationary value)."
                },
                "P_O3": {
                    "type": "unknown",
                    "units": "m^-3*s^-1",
                    "description": "Net ozone production rate [m⁻³ s⁻¹] (Eq. 6.8). The difference between the NO₂-photolysis ozone source and the NO+O₃ ozone sink: P_O3 = j_NO2·[NO₂] − k_NO_O3·[NO]·[O₃]. Positive when the system is below photostationary state (ambient O₃ < O3_pss → net production), negative when above (net loss), and zero at exact PSS — P_O3 and Φ are two views of the same departure from equilibrium (P_O3 = j_NO2·[NO₂]·(1 − 1/Φ)). At the typical-midday defaults P_O3 ≈ -7.5e13 m⁻³/s (net loss, since the default O3 exceeds O3_pss). This is the chemical tendency the NO-NO₂-O₃ cycle alone would impose on ozone; the full tropospheric tendency adds the peroxy-radical channels that NOxPhotochemistry deliberately omits."
                }
            },
            "equations": [
